00:59:18,530 --> 00:59:28,330
And in order to play with interference and to play with diffraction and be able to manipulate exactly what is the interference pattern

618
00:59:28,330 --> 00:59:35,610
that I get on the wafer, I need to make sure that this is spatially coherent light.

619
00:59:35,610 --> 00:59:37,150
And how do we do that.

620
00:59:37,150 --> 00:59:40,190
We create this so-called 4f system.

621
00:59:40,190 --> 00:59:45,990
A guy Kohler invented this by having this four lens system.

622
00:59:45,990 --> 00:59:55,230
What I can create is, for every point in the light source, there is one particular set of planar waves that hits the mask.

623
00:59:55,230 --> 01:00:04,550
So if I imagine myself sitting on the mask and I look back at the illumination, imagine a circle

624
01:00:04,550 --> 01:00:07,570
and I could get light from that whole circle at me.

625
01:00:07,570 --> 01:00:09,750
That would be a cone of light.

626
01:00:09,750 --> 01:00:13,250
I could have light just coming from the center of that point.

627
01:00:13,250 --> 01:00:15,710
That would be one planar wave.

628
01:00:15,710 --> 01:00:22,330
I will also show you that I can have light coming from there and from there, and then I get very particular diffraction.

629
01:00:22,330 --> 01:00:25,250
And that's all the optimization that we're going to do.

630
01:00:28,270 --> 01:00:35,780
So here you see that a little bit for every point in my source plane in the illumination I now get a number.

631
01:00:35,780 --> 01:00:41,820
I got plane waves onto the mask that leads to diffraction.

632
01:00:41,820 --> 01:00:45,040
So the diffraction orders are captured by the objective lens.

633
01:00:45,040 --> 01:00:48,400
And the bigger this lens, the bigger these opening angles,

634
01:00:48,400 --> 01:00:56,000
the more of these diffraction patterns I capture, and the better will be the reconstruction of the image here on the image plane.

635
01:00:56,000 --> 01:00:58,980
That's basically Fourier optics.

636
01:00:58,980 --> 01:01:09,420
So let's look a little bit more at diffraction for those of you that are not totally familiar with that, Mr. Huygens already said, hey, when you have light hitting an opening,

637
01:01:09,420 --> 01:01:14,900
each point here starts to act as a source of spherical waves,

638
01:01:14,900 --> 01:01:26,000
and it's really the path optical path length differences that then create this pattern here, where in some directions I end up getting light, and in other directions I do not get light.

639
01:01:26,000 --> 01:01:28,900
And those are called the diffraction orders.

640
01:01:28,900 --> 01:01:37,440
In essence, if I look at my aperture, the mask, and I think of that as a repeating pattern.

641
01:01:37,440 --> 01:01:44,880
I can take the Fourier transform of that pattern, and every diffraction order encodes one of the Fourier components.

642
01:01:44,880 --> 01:01:47,640
And you can think of the lens as a filter.

643
01:01:47,640 --> 01:01:51,700
How many of the Fourier components can I capture that lead.

644
01:01:51,700 --> 01:01:54,660
Help me reconstruct the pattern of the mask.

645
01:01:54,660 --> 01:01:58,120
Now on the wafer.

646
01:01:58,120 --> 01:02:04,320
So in certain directions, look at this wave from this opening.

